  "name": "Montoir 35.7 m LNG pool fire (1987)",
  "citation": "Nedelka, D., Moorhouse, J., Tucker, R.F. (1989), reported in: Raj, P.K. (2005). Large LNG Fire Thermal Radiation - Modeling Issues & Hazard Criteria Revisited. Process Safety Progress 24(3), 192-202. Table 1, 'Summary of LNG Fire Experiments'. Flame length read off Figure 4 of: Wang, C.J., Wen, J.X., Chen, Z.B. (2014). Simulation of Large-Scale LNG Pool Fires Using FireFOAM. Combustion Science and Technology 186(10-11), 1632-1649. DOI 10.1080/00102202.2014.935615.",
  "dataConfidence": "Medium",
  "description": "Three LNG fires on a 1000 m2 insulated concrete dike (35.7 m equivalent diameter) at Montoir-de-Bretagne, the largest land LNG spill fire tests to date. Raj (2005) p.5 summarises Nedelka et al.: average burning rate 0.14 kg/m2s (regression 3.3e-4 m/s, 7.1 MW/m2); narrow-angle spot emissive powers 290-320 kW/m2; and the equivalent surface emissive power computed over the ENTIRE VISIBLE FIRE AREA of 257-273 kW/m2. That last definition is the one this model uses, which makes the comparison direct. Cross wind 9.6 m/s per Table 2 of Wang et al. (2014).",
